WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.810
SPEAKER: Service accounts--
where do they come from?

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:06.510
In the last episode, I mentioned
that there are some service

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.189
accounts that are
created automatically

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:10.860
for us, like Google-managed
service accounts and default

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:12.420
service accounts.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:14.340
That's really nice,
but you might want

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:16.650
to know how to create your own.

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:19.320
After that, how will
you manage them?

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:22.710
What will you do when you
need to get rid of one?

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:24.480
These questions
mean that it's time

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.520
to talk about what's what
with creating, managing,

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:29.700
and retiring your
service accounts.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:33.330
Creating a service account is
as easy as one gcloud command--

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:36.870
gcloud iam
service-accounts create.

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:38.910
Upon creation, this
new service account

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:42.150
will have no rules assigned,
so zero access to any APIs

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:43.720
and services.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:46.290
Before we talk about assigning
roles and permissions,

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:50.190
let's iron out the creation
process a little bit more.

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:53.050
Every service account
should have a purpose.

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:56.250
One of the best best practices
that you could practice

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.500
is to make sure a service
account's purpose is

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:01.050
clear to you and to any
other administrator that

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:03.640
might inherit your
project in the future.

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:05.430
So when you create
a service account,

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:09.120
make sure that you include
a descriptive display name.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:10.860
If you want to take
it a step further,

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:13.680
and I recommend that you
do, you can include even

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:18.600
more details and context in that
service account's description.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.310
When creating service
accounts, make

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:22.470
it a habit to figure out
a meaningful display name

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:25.830
and description on creation by
tacking on the --display-name

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:29.670
and --description flags
to your gcloud command.

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:32.250
This will be especially useful
if your project accumulates

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:35.430
a long list of service accounts,
each with a different purpose

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:39.390
and set of IAM roles
assigned to it.

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:42.330
You can view the display name
and description for any service

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:45.750
account using gcloud iam
service-accounts describe,

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:48.960
followed by the
service account email.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.300
You can always update
these fields in the console

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:56.002
or by using the CLI with gcloud
iam service-accounts update.

00:01:56.002 --> 00:01:57.960
When you have service
accounts in your project,

00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.970
a big part of managing
them is managing

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:03.540
the IAM roles and permissions
associated with them.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:06.336
If you're already familiar with
Google Cloud's IAM policies,

00:02:06.336 --> 00:02:08.669
then you might already know
that checking what a service

00:02:08.669 --> 00:02:11.039
account is capable of doing
is as simple as checking

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:13.770
the IAM policy of your project.

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:16.890
gcloud projects
get-iam-policy followed

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:19.620
by your project ID will
show you all the IAM role

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:22.080
bindings on your project,
including the roles assigned

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:24.510
to your service accounts.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:26.238
Service account
management doesn't only

00:02:26.238 --> 00:02:28.530
mean understanding what
resources service accounts have

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:31.890
access to, but also who
has access to your service

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:32.940
accounts.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:35.220
When you're taking a
look at your IAM policy,

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:36.960
take note of any
accounts that have

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:40.230
roles associated with managing
or using service accounts.

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:43.200
This includes predefined roles
like Service Account Token

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:47.400
Creator, Service Account Admin,
and Service Account Key Admin.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.140
Some of these roles
will allow users

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:52.840
to make changes to, or even
act as, service accounts.

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:55.440
That means it's technically
possible for a user

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:57.870
that you intend to be
restricted to have unrestricted

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:02.250
permissions via a service
account with bloated access.

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:06.150
It's a security topic that
we'll cover in a later episode.

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:08.610
In addition to the IAM
policy on your project,

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:11.010
there is also an IAM
policy for each service

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:13.210
account in your project.

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:14.790
The role bindings
on these policies

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:17.310
are limited specifically to
their corresponding service

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:22.020
account, and are not visible
on the project's IAM policy.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:24.120
You can retrieve a particular
service accounts IAM

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:26.970
policy with gcloud
iam service-accounts

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:30.390
get-iam-policy, followed
by the account email.

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:32.310
All of this role
and policy info is

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:36.130
available from the Cloud Console
under IAM and Admin Settings.

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:38.130
There may come a day when
you'll want to get rid

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:39.870
of a service account.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:40.950
You could just delete it.

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:44.400
But beware-- once you do,
any users, applications,

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.790
or VMs that are configured to
use it to access any resources

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:49.860
will lose that access.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:52.320
It is important to use caution
when deleting a service

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:54.060
account, especially
if you're not so

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.050
sure about what it's used for.

00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:59.040
It's much safer to
disable it first.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:01.522
Once an account is
disabled, yeah, applications

00:04:01.522 --> 00:04:03.480
will lose that access
that they had through it,

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:05.010
just like when you delete it.

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:07.530
But a disabled service
account can easily

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:09.900
be re-enabled at any time.

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:12.150
Does that mean that when you
delete a service account,

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:13.800
it's completely,
totally, utterly,

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:16.740
and hopelessly lost forever?

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:18.360
No, not exactly.

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:21.269
You could undelete a
deleted service account,

00:04:21.269 --> 00:04:24.420
but only if two
things are true--

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:27.270
one, the service account
had to have been deleted

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:30.300
within the past
30 days, and two,

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:32.310
there can be no other
service accounts

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:35.040
in existence on your
project with the same name

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.290
as the deleted service account.

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:39.360
That's because disabling
a service account

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:42.420
will reserve the name,
but deleting it will not.

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:45.060
After you check the service
account's details and roles

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:46.740
and decide it's
probably not in use,

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:49.770
you can disable the service
account with gcloud iam

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:52.250
service-accounts disable.

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:54.420
If it turns out that the
account is needed again

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:56.780
for whatever reason,
enabling it is as simple

00:04:56.780 --> 00:04:59.900
as gcloud iam
service-accounts enable.

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:02.180
Otherwise, you can
delete it with gcloud iam

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:04.430
service-accounts delete.

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:07.280
So we talked about how we can
best create service accounts

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:09.020
by providing them
with a clear purpose,

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:12.380
and how to safely delete
them by disabling them first.

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:14.240
We also talked about
managing their access

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:17.990
by checking the project and
service account IAM policies.

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:21.410
That's only part of what goes
into managing service accounts.

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:24.290
Next time we'll expand on
that, and talk a bit more

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:27.530
about securing service accounts'
roles and IAM policies.

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:28.490
See you then.

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:31.840
[MUSIC PLAYING]

